An unreadable go.mod beside a healthy module. Aborting here would be the wrong answer twice
over: the rest of the tree is still worth reporting, and a module that cannot be read is a fact
the report has to carry — the same distinction as a package whose tests panicked versus one that
has none.

go list -m at the root is unaffected: a nested module is not in the root's graph.

modules_on_disk: 2
go_list_m: 1
go_list_dotdotdot: 1
discovered_modules: 2
discovered_packages: 1
packages_with_tests: 0
has_workspace: 0
modules_in_workspace: 0
modules_with_errors: 1
-- go.mod --
module broken.test

go 1.27
-- root.go --
package broken

func R() int { return 1 }
-- bad/go.mod --
this is not a go.mod
-- bad/bad.go --
package bad

func B() int { return 2 }
